   "source": [
    "## Step 2: Crop a small \"postage stamp\" around the lens\n",
    "\n",
    "The full JWST mosaic covers a huge area of sky (thousands of galaxies).\n",
    "Gravitational lens modeling is computationally done on a small cutout\n",
    "centered on just the one system we care about -- both because it's much\n",
    "faster to compute, and because the lens equation we'll use later assumes a\n",
    "single dominant foreground mass, not a whole crowded field.\n",
    "\n",
    "We use the FITS file's **WCS (World Coordinate System)** -- essentially a\n",
    "built-in \"translation table\" between pixel positions in the image and real\n",
    "sky coordinates (RA/Dec) -- to find exactly which pixels correspond to our\n",
    "target's sky position, then cut out a small square around it.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
