    "## Step 3: Build a PSF (Point Spread Function) and save data in PyAutoLens's format\n",
    "\n",
    "**What is a PSF, and why do we need one?** No telescope is perfect -- even a\n",
    "single, infinitely small point of light (like a distant star) gets slightly\n",
    "smeared out by diffraction and optics when captured by a real instrument. The\n",
    "PSF describes exactly *how* a point of light gets blurred by this particular\n",
    "telescope+instrument+filter combination. PyAutoLens needs to know this\n",
    "because, when it builds a *model* image of the lensed source, it must blur\n",
    "that model by the same PSF before comparing it to your real (also blurred)\n",
    "data -- otherwise the comparison would be unfair.\n",
    "\n",
    "Here, we approximate the PSF as a 2D Gaussian (bell-curve) shape, using the\n",
    "telescope's theoretical diffraction limit for this filter as its width. This\n",
    "is a simplification -- a real PSF (e.g. from WebbPSF, a NASA-provided tool\n",
    "that models JWST's actual optics) would be more accurate, but a Gaussian is a\n",
    "reasonable starting point.\n"
   ]
